  "summary": "4096 colors, tracker modules and Workbench grey — the machine that taught Europe to paint on pixels.",
  "description": "The Commodore Amiga defined a creative-computing visual culture: Deluxe Paint pixel illustration, HAM-mode 4096-color showpieces, Boing Ball demo physics, and Workbench's grey-and-blue desktop look. TV stations (like BBC's 1990s graphics departments) used Amigas for broadcast art; demoscene groups (Kefrens, Red Sector) pushed copper bars and dot pools; and tracked MOD music gave the machine a sound identity. The Amiga look — chunky fonts, saturated pixel gradients, cheerful gadget chrome — persists in retro-computing revivals, PiStorm-era hardware and A-EON continuations, and Deluxe Paint-era brushes seeded today's pixel-art tooling.",
  "culturalContext": "The Amiga defined Europe's creative-computing identity: broadcast graphics, demoscene masterworks and Deluxe Paint artistry made it the \"multimedia\" machine of the 1980s-90s, and its revival communities (PiStorm hardware, Amiga Forever archives) keep Workbench grey and copper bars as retrocomputing's warmest emblem.",
